    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d336">

                <pb facs="0473" n="451" sameAs="frus:frus1951v02/pg451"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/2–252: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chairman of the United States Delegation to
                        the General Assembly</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Roosevelt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1952-02-02T21:00:00+01:00">February 2, 1952—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Delga 1291. Re membership. Kyrou<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d336-f1"/> plans to call SC meeting
                    Feb 5 on membership.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d336-f2"/> He proposes to have SC consider Fr draft on admission of Italy and Sov
                    omnibus draft. He feels SC shld not at this time
                    consider Pak draft on Libya or Peruvian res approved by GA.</p>
                <p>Laskey<anchor n="3" xml:id="d336-f3"/> (UK) says they feel it preferable to have
                    brief remarks here without vote, SC then
                    deferring further discussion until NY. UK wishes based partly on fear Itals
                    after Sov veto will go straight to GA, asking for
                    admission there, partly on dislike of prolonged SC debate here leading foregone conclusion. He says UK may suggest
                    continuation of discussion in NY on ground desire for consultation among
                    permanent members as suggested in last para Peruvian draft as adopted by GA.</p>
                <p>Laskey says UK wld also prefer to have Libya considered and if necessary voted on
                    along with others rather than referring it to com on new members.</p>
                <p>
                    Dept’s views requested on consultations with
                    Kyrou and other members on tactics to be followed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Roosevelt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d336-f1" n="1" xml:id="d336-f1-content">
                <p>Alexis Kyrou, Permanent Representative of Greece to the United Nations, President
                    of the Security Council for the month of February 1952.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d336-f2" n="2" xml:id="d336-f2-content">
                <p>The last formal action of the Security Council on this matter took place on
                    December 18 and 19, 1951; see editorial note, <ref target="#pg410">p.
                    410</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d336-f3" n="3" xml:id="d336-f3-content">
                <p>D.S. Laskey, Secretary-General of the Delegation of the United Kingdom.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
